      "retrieved": "2026-10-12",
      "revision": 1357664161,
      "text": "Rubus deliciosus is a North American species of flowering plant in the rose family. Common names include the delicious raspberry, boulder raspberry, Rocky Mountain raspberry or snowy bramble."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Rubus deliciosus」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rubus_deliciosus#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-12",
        "revision": 1357664161,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "Rubus deliciosus requires well drained soil in full sun. It is hardy to USDA zone #5 (-20 °F; -29 °C). It is propagated by cuttings in July or August.\n\n■ Hybrids\nIt has been hybridized in cultivation with the very closely related (and possibly conspecific) Rubus trilobus of southern Mexico. This hybrid was introduced by the English plant collector Collingwood Ingram as R. × tridel (Flowering bramble). It is popular as an ornamental plant in gardens, having larger flowers up to 8 cm diameter. The cultivar R. × tridel 'Benenden' has gained the Royal Horticultural Society's Award of Garden Merit.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
